fact WorkItem(id: "WI-991", description: some(value: "SCALAND: `SimpleTermStore.spanOf` IS A MUTATOR WEARING AN ACCESSOR'S NAME, AND THE TEST IT EXISTS FOR IS HELD UP BY STATEMENT ORDER. WI-964 made `spanOf` increment a `spanReads` counter so `ParseSpanGrowthTest` can measure that type lowering reads children's spans back instead of recomputing them. The counter is the only observable the test has, and `spanOf` is on the loader's per-term path, so every reader anywhere moves it. Concretely: `ParseSpanGrowthTest` snapshots `spanReads` and then calls `SpanFixture.fnSpans`, which reads spans through the SAME counting accessor. Hoisting that `val` above the snapshot — an ordinary readability edit — leaves both the floor assertion and the ratio bound passing while they now measure the WALK rather than the parse, so the quadratic the test exists to catch could be restored with the suite green. The same erosion follows from routing `nameBearingWithoutSpan` / `spansEndingInWhitespace` through the accessor (they index `spans` raw today) or from adding a growth assertion to `ParseSpanCoverageTest` — measured: a stdlib parse is 0 span reads, but `Loader.loadAll` is 912, so loader reads would swamp the signal. WORK: make the observable insensitive to who else reads. A `protected def onRead()` overridden by a counting test subclass, or a store factory parameter on `Parser.parse`, keeps the measurement while taking the write off the production path. ACCEPTANCE: reorder the statements in `ParseSpanGrowthTest` so the walk precedes the snapshot and show the test still measures the parse — that reordering is the regression this ticket exists to make impossible. Say at the site which test fails when the change is backed out. Found by code review (2026-08-04). Related: WI-964, WI-965."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "scaland-sbt-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-08-04T19:22:34Z"))

fact Tag(workitem: "WI-991", name: "scaland")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-991", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED. The counter is off SimpleTermStore entirely: `spanOf` is a plain accessor (the class is now `open` for that ONE override, allocators `final`), and a count is taken PER PARSE by a counting subclass in the test tree — `SpanFixture.parseCountingSpanReads` hands it back with the file, so no later reader and no statement order can move it. `ParseSpanGrowthTest` now walks BEFORE it asserts (the ticket's acceptance) and reads 700/1400, unchanged; it also pins those counts exactly, which is the control for THIS ticket — the backed-out shape reads 800 there and stays green on every other assertion. LANDMINE, found by code review after a first WRONG shape: the seam must NOT be a public `terms` parameter guarded by `require(terms.size == 0)`. Size is a proxy for freshness, not freshness — MEASURED, `namespace d end` allocates no terms, so a store that had already backed a declaration-only parse passed the guard and two files aliased one store, silently. The shape that works is `private[anthill] parseInto(source, fileName, terms)` with the public `parse` minting its own store: freshness by construction on every production path, and no guard that can be wrong. Two corrections to this ticket's own text: the reorder ALONE does not let the quadratic back in (10600 against 41000 is ratio 3.87, still over the 2.5 ceiling) — what it breaks is what the number MEANS; and the '912' figure is a whole-corpus total over 58 stdlib files (largest single file 97), not per file, and the loader never touched this test's stores at all. Only readers of the SAME store instance can contaminate a count.", at: ?at)
